--- abstract

The Agent Mailbox Access Protocol (AMAP) is a wire contract, carried over a
filesystem namespace, between an untrusted per-agent connector and a trusted,
credentialed mail runtime. It gives a sandboxed AI agent the ability to send
and receive email through a runtime that does not trust it: every outbound
message begins as an inert request file that the runtime copies into private
staging, validates independently, and applies policy to before anything is
transmitted, and every inbound message is authenticated and screened by the
runtime before a notice describing it appears in the agent's namespace. This
document specifies the directory layout and its filesystem obligations —
ownership, write and delete rights, and the path, symlink, and concurrency
discipline a runtime observes; the JSON message shapes for submit requests,
results, deliver notices, spooled message bodies, and an optional binding
record; the correlation and versioning rules, including a direction-aware
unknown-member tolerance rule; and two conformance classes. AMAP is
deliberately not a mail protocol: it neither defines nor replaces SMTP, IMAP,
or provider-side infrastructure, and composes with them rather than
displacing them. Its single design center is that no security property
depends on the agent — or on the connector speaking for it — behaving
correctly.

**Naming.** This protocol was called AMP ("Agent Mailbox Protocol") until
2026-09-15. It was renamed because AMP collides with the IETF DTN working
group's adopted `draft-ietf-dtn-amp`. The filename of this draft is unchanged
at this document version; the schema identifiers moved with the name (Change
Log, 3.1.0).

