# Conformance Suite {#app-h}

*(Normative for the classes of [](#sec-10-3); the honesty statement below
is part of the specification.)*

The suite consists of golden artifacts under `fixtures/valid/` and
`fixtures/invalid/`, plus a validator, `fixtures/validate.py`.

{::include-nested draft/build/appendix-h-roster.mkd}

**Running it.** The validator is stdlib-only and takes no arguments:

~~~ text
python3 fixtures/validate.py
~~~

It exits 0 if and only if every fixture under `valid/` passes and every
fixture under `invalid/` fails. It requires no counterpart, no network, and
no dependency: **the fixtures are the other side.**

**How a fixture is mapped to a schema.** By filename prefix — `request-` to
the submit-request schema, `notice-` to deliver-notice, `result-` to
result, `message-` to inbound-message, `identity-` to binding-record, and
`peer-` to peer-notice ([](#app-f)).

**What the validator checks.** Schema validation in the JSON Schema
2020-12 dialect {{JSON-SCHEMA-2020-12}}, over the subset of keywords these
schemas use (`type`, `const`, `enum`, `minLength`, `pattern`, `minItems`,
`minimum`, `items`, `required`, `properties`, `additionalProperties`,
`allOf`, and a single `if`/`then` pair) — plus two **named post-checks**,
neither expressible in standard JSON Schema:

- the Result-side binding `attachments[i].index == i`, contiguous and
  unique over `0` through `n-1` ([](#sec-6-1));
- the `content_ref` index and prefix binding on Notice, Inbound Message,
  and peer-notice documents: every Descriptor carrying `content_ref` must
  equal `<notice_id>.attachments/<i>` for its own array position `i`,
  binding both the trailing index and the leading prefix ([](#sec-7-2)).

A Connector conformance claim requires running **both** the schema
validation and the second post-check, because exactly one invalid
`notice-*` fixture — and its `peer-*` counterpart — is schema-valid on its
own and is caught only by that post-check ([](#app-g), [](#sec-10-3)).

**A note on pattern anchoring, and a portability cost.** Every schema
pattern in this suite that ends in `$` is written as `(?!\n)$`. The reason
is that a bare `$` matches before a single trailing newline in several
widely used regex engines — Python's `re`, Java, and .NET among them —
unlike the ECMA-262 dialect JSON Schema specifies. Since every one of these
patterns exists to forbid exactly the control characters a permissive `$`
would let through at the end of a string, the lookahead closes that gap in
the schemas themselves rather than in any one validator. Seven fixtures pin
the trailing-newline case specifically, distinct from the CRLF fixtures,
which embed `\r\n` and were already rejected even under a permissive
engine.

The cost is disclosed rather than discovered: **lookahead-less engines
cannot compile these patterns at all.** RE2 (Go's `regexp`), Rust's
`regex` crate, and POSIX ERE reject `(?!` outright, so the three schemas
carrying the guard fail to load rather than validating loosely. Those
engines already treat `$` as strict end-of-string and never had the gap,
so an implementer on one of them should strip the lookahead — not add a
check outside the pattern, which would be both unnecessary and not what
breaks. The failure is loud (a compile error, never silent acceptance).

**What a green run proves.** That every document shape this protocol
defines is accepted or rejected as specified, by an implementation's own
parser, with no counterpart present.

**What a green run does NOT prove.** The following are filesystem and
policy invariants that no document validator can observe. They are
obligations of the conformance classes regardless ([](#sec-10-3)), and are
checked operationally:

- Sidecar file existence — an extra file, a missing file, or a directory
  present when the Descriptor array is empty or absent.
- `size_bytes` and `sha256` actually matching the bytes on disk.
- `filename` display-sanitization, and the "never a path" invariant.
- The binding between an ordinal and a directory entry on disk. The schema
  checks only the *Descriptor* array's own index member, not that
  directory entry `N` exists.
- Inbound Sidecar directories keyed on the Runtime-minted notice
  identifier rather than the provider `message.id`.
- The location of Runtime-private Staging, outside the mounted Volume.
- Write ordering and commit-sentinel atomicity — Sidecars before
  `req-<id>.json`; `.tmp` plus rename.
- Caps and allowlists — type allowlists, size caps, Volume quotas.
- The absence of a spooled body for a quarantined or blocked message
  (enforcement by absence, [](#sec-7-3)).
- A Connector's tolerance of a read-only `inbound/` tree. The prescribed
  check is operational, not a fixture: run the Connector against an
  inbound tree it cannot write and confirm that it starts, relays every
  pending Notice, resolves bodies, and submits outbound requests, without
  requiring any inbound write to succeed ([](#sec-10-3)).
- Ingest by copy — validation, policy evaluation, and composition running
  over a Runtime-private snapshot taken before any of those steps, rather
  than re-reading an Agent-writable path ([](#sec-4-1)).
- Write-side path discipline, all three conjunctive legs: full
  parent-chain resolution through pinned directory descriptors from the
  Namespace root; each directory component opened `O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY`
  at the moment it is added to that chain; and the final component created
  `O_EXCL` and never followed, failing closed on `EEXIST` and `ELOOP`. Any
  one leg alone is a known-insufficient partial mitigation ([](#sec-4-1)).
- Read-side open discipline — per-component no-symlink-follow opens,
  non-blocking opens, `fstat`-based regular-file verification through the
  open descriptor, and bounded reads and caps on every Agent-influenced
  path ([](#sec-4-1)).
- Single writer and single drainer per Namespace — an operational and
  deployment invariant, such as an OS-level lock held for the process
  lifetime ([](#sec-4-1)).
- Compose-time header-boundary stripping: even where the schemas'
  control-character patterns are bypassed upstream, the Runtime rejects or
  strips CR, LF, and NUL before composing the outbound message
  ([](#sec-5-1)).
- The peer-origin profile's operational obligations (v3.1.0 DRAFT), none
  of them visible to a document validator: the write-authority partition —
  no component that admits messages from a transport not restricted to
  authorised peer senders holds write authority over `peer/`, and the
  prescribed check is a write attempt from inside the admitting component
  that must fail; intake isolation for shared-transport peer candidates, in
  a location that is neither `inbound/` nor `peer/` and unreachable from
  any Agent Namespace; verification placement in a component holding peer
  write authority that hashes body and attachment bytes itself;
  `sender_exposure` never sender-settable; a peer notice carrying
  `in_reply_to` only when the placing Runtime resolved it from its own
  record, an unresolvable key being refused rather than stripped; and
  at-most-once action by the Connector, with an ambiguous target held
  rather than fanned out.

Beyond these, the behavioral obligations of Sections {{<sec-7}} and
{{<sec-8}} — the untrusted-content posture, the treatment of `task_id` as
correlation rather than authorization, and the requirement that verified
sender identity never be taken from `message.from` — sit outside the
fixture gate entirely. They constrain what an implementation *does* with a
valid document, which no document-level gate can see.

# A Deferred Seam-Visible "Handled" Signal (Non-Normative) {#app-i}

**This appendix is non-normative and is not in force at this document
version.** It records the reasoning behind a gap deliberately left open,
not an addition to the protocol. Nothing below is a directory, a member, or
an artifact that a conforming implementation can rely on, and no
requirement keyword appears anywhere in it.

**The gap.** Making inbound consumption-tracking Connector-private
([](#sec-4-1)) closes the writability conflict but leaves a knowledge and
capability split unbridged: only the Connector knows that a Notice has
been relayed to its Agent, while on a read-only inbound tree only the
Runtime can delete anything. Neither side alone can turn "consumed" into
"reclaimed."

**A sketch, not a specification.** One shape that would bridge it is a
Connector-written, zero-byte marker per acknowledged Notice, written into
`outbound/`, where the Connector already has write access in every
posture:

~~~ text
outbound/acks/notice-<id>   # sketch only - not part of the protocol
~~~
{: #ex-ack-sketch title="Sketch only — not part of this protocol"}

A Runtime could then delete the acknowledged Notice and its spooled
message, and finally the marker itself. This is presented purely as a
sketch: no directory and no wire member is introduced by this appendix.

**Why a Connector-forgeable signal would be acceptable here.** A
garbage-collection signal is a resource decision rather than a security
decision. An Agent that falsely claims consumption loses only its own
unread mail; one that never claims consumption only bloats its own inbox.
Neither crosses a trust boundary that matters, and neither touches any
record of the exchange the Runtime keeps on its own side. That is what
distinguishes this case from every other "the Agent writes it, so it
cannot be trusted" case in this document — but it is a distinction a
future version would need to state explicitly rather than assume.

**Why a high-water mark cannot substitute.** [](#sec-8) requires `task_id`
to be unguessable and never sequential, and nothing in this document
orders notice identifiers, so no single "processed through N" cursor is
expressible. Any consumption signal has to be a *set* of identifiers, or
time-ordered — and time-ordering would require the consumer to process
strictly in delivery order, which nothing today requires.

**The lifecycle this would enable, and its boundaries.** A reliable "is
this one done?" answer is the watermark a real retention lifecycle
promotes against: cycling a working set, rolling handled traffic into
period-compacted archives, then expiring or tiering to cold storage. Three
boundaries would need to hold if this is ever specified.

- **The seam is not the record.** A Runtime that needs an exchange of
  record already keeps one outside this protocol, tamper-evident.
  Retention here is about an Agent's access to its own history, not about
  the durability of evidence; conflating the two produces an archive that
  satisfies neither.
- **An Agent-writable archive is never evidence.** A compacted archive
  living under `outbound/`, which is Agent-writable in every posture, can
  be rewritten by the Agent it belongs to. Anything meant to be relied on
  has to be Runtime-written or kept outside this protocol entirely.
- **It must not grow the read surface.** The read available today is a
  point lookup from a notice identifier to a body. "Search my archive" is
  a materially larger capability — decompression, indexing, query — for a
  deliberately dumb, credential-free relay to take on. If archives are
  ever exposed to an Agent, that likely belongs behind a Runtime-served
  surface rather than the file-drop seam.

**Why this is deferred rather than specified now.** A capability starts
with a change to this document once a second implementation independently
wants it, with members and fixtures proposed together. Today exactly one
reference Connector carries a private workaround for the underlying
problem — a writability-probing fallback with three modes: move, ledger,
and in-memory. There is no second data point yet to generalize from, and
specifying now would freeze one side's convention as the protocol's
answer.

**One honesty note for a future implementer.** The sketch's own
garbage-collection step is not atomic: if a Runtime crashes between
deleting the Notice artifacts and deleting the acknowledgment marker, the
marker is orphaned, pointing at a Notice that no longer exists. Any real
design needs idempotent collection, in which a missing target is not an
error — recorded here so that it is not rediscovered from scratch.

