## 3.1.0 (DRAFT) — the protocol is AMAP; the outbound extension space; the peer-origin profile's schema
{: numbered="false"}

An additive **minor** bump; the wire major stays `"2"`.

- **The protocol is AMAP; the schema `$id` authority is `amap-spec`**
  (2026-09-15). The protocol formerly called AMP ("Agent Mailbox Protocol")
  is renamed AMAP ("Agent Mailbox Access Protocol") because AMP collides
  with the IETF DTN working group's adopted `draft-ietf-dtn-amp`; the
  specification repository moves from `agent-mailbox-protocol` to
  `amap-spec` with it. The six schema `$id` values move from
  `https://agent-mailbox-protocol/schemas/…` to `https://amap-spec/schemas/…`.
  Nothing on the wire changes shape: no document carries the protocol's name
  as a field, and `$id` is an identifier the gate never dereferences.
  Implementations that mirror the Runtime's address spell its local part
  `amap.router` from this version. This draft's filename is a separate
  decision and is unchanged here. This version is marked DRAFT: its profile
  prose is a companion document (`spec/peer-origin.md`, itself DRAFT), and
  its schema and fixtures may still change before it is settled.
- **Outbound extension space.** `outbound/ext/<name>/` is defined as a
  Connector-owned side channel a Runtime MAY read by out-of-band agreement,
  with no shape specified and nothing security-relevant permitted to depend
  on it ([](#sec-4-1)). `<name>` is pinned as a stable Connector id —
  chosen once, unchanged by any rename of the Connector's repository,
  package, or deployment, and opaque to the Runtime that reads it; the
  wording it replaced ("a Connector or deployment name") permitted a
  repository name to leak into a wire-visible path. The first id is
  `claude-code`. One normative clarification sits beside it: an outcome
  written there is a claim by the receiving side, never proof of delivery
  or of non-delivery, and the Runtime's own delivery record is
  authoritative over it ([](#sec-11-15)). There is deliberately no inbound
  extension space.
- **Peer-notice schema.** The peer-origin profile's artifact is pinned by
  `peer-notice.schema.json` ([](#app-f)): the deliver-notice layout with
  `kind` and `mailbox` fixed to `peer`, `message.from` a bare addr-spec,
  message identifiers in the notice-identifier character set, and the
  optional Runtime-asserted `sender_exposure` stamp with its required
  members. The profile's prose is the companion document
  `spec/peer-origin.md` (v3.1.0 DRAFT).
- **Fixtures: 75 total (27 valid, 48 invalid), up from 58.** 16 `peer-*`
  fixtures were added (6 valid, 10 invalid), covering a minimal notice, a
  reply, the exposure stamp asserted both ways, a clean attachment
  reference, an unknown-member case, and rejections for a `deliver` kind
  under the peer tree, a `peer` kind under the inbound tree, a display-name
  or CRLF `from`, encoded message identifiers, a missing `asserted_by`, a
  missing last-delivery timestamp when exposure is asserted true, an inbox
  `mailbox`, a `content_ref` index mismatch, and a trailing-newline
  identifier. The validator maps the `peer-` prefix to the new schema and
  applies the `content_ref` post-check to it. Three existing fixtures had
  example strings normalised to `example.*` identities (one free-text body,
  two addresses in injection examples); each stays valid or invalid for the
  same reason as before, and no schema changed.

## 3.0.0 — direction-aware tolerance, and six closed safety holes
{: numbered="false"}

A **major document bump**; the wire major stays `"2"`. [](#sec-10-1) states
why the two numbers diverge here, and why bumping the wire would have
caused the exact harm this version exists to repair.

- **Unknown-member tolerance, direction-aware.** The three Runtime-authored
  artifacts — deliver-notice, inbound-message, and result — became **open**
  at every level: `additionalProperties: false` was removed from the top
  level, from `message`, from Descriptor items, and from `attestation`, and
  a consumer must tolerate an unrecognized member. Emitting one remains
  producer non-conformance. The Agent-authored submit-request envelope
  stays **closed** at every level. Consequence: adding an optional member
  to a Runtime-authored artifact is now genuinely additive; adding any
  member to the submit-request is a major change.
- **...and for values.** The `enum` constraint was removed from
  `message.sender_standing` and `message.provenance`, the two explicitly
  advisory vocabularies, and a consumer must tolerate an unrecognized value
  in either. `kind`, `outcome` (both levels), and `disposition` remain
  closed, enum-locked vocabularies.
- **Ingest by copy.** A Runtime must copy an Agent-authored request and its
  Sidecars into private staging before validating, must act only on that
  copy, and must not re-read an Agent-writable path after a decision
  depending on its content ([](#sec-4-1)).
- **Address syntax and header-injection discipline.** A new obligation in
  [](#sec-5-1) plus schema patterns on `draft.to`/`cc`, `draft.subject`,
  attachment `filename` and `media_type`, `draft.reply_to_message_id`,
  `in_reply_to`, `agent_id`, and `ts` — including the two members composed
  directly into headers, which earlier schema coverage missed.
- **Path discipline, write- and read-side both.** The write-side rule
  became **conjunctive** ([](#sec-4-1)), and a new read-side rule applies
  the equivalent discipline to every Agent-influenceable read. A
  `notice_id` character-set pattern was added to the deliver-notice and
  inbound-message schemas so that every legal `notice_id` is also a legal
  `content_ref` prefix. **Disclosed cost:** `notice_id` was previously
  unconstrained, so a Runtime minting identifiers outside the new character
  set — an ISO-8601 timestamp, standard base64, a URN, or anything else
  carrying punctuation outside it — now emits Notices and Inbound Messages
  that fail schema validation on a *pattern violation on a required
  member*, and a conforming Connector drops them. **Migration:** such a
  Runtime must re-mint identifiers under the new character set (unpadded
  base64url or lowercase hex, for instance) before any Connector it talks
  to upgrades, because the failure is otherwise silent on both sides.
- **Single writer, single drainer.** [](#sec-4-1) now names the
  concurrency assumption that every atomicity, commit-sentinel, and
  `req_id` rule already depended on.
- **`message.from` named untrusted.** The untrusted-content sentence now
  covers explicitly the member most likely to be rendered to a human or
  used for a trust judgement ([](#sec-7-1)).
- **Document authority.** The contract, its schemas, and its fixtures are
  normative; this rendering is generated from them and is
  non-authoritative where they disagree. The conformance-class text was
  moved into the contract itself so that the demotion deletes nothing, and
  a stray claim of a second, message-specific Sidecar directory was
  corrected to match the layout tree.
- **Corrections shipped with this version**, rather than a tidied history:
  (i) the write rule described in an earlier revision as "strengthened"
  had in fact been weakened — the exclusive-create requirement on the
  final component had been deleted rather than supplemented
  ([](#sec-10-1)); (ii) the inbound drop rule described as a pure closure
  over schema validation was refuted by this version's own
  `content_ref`-binding fixture, and is now stated as two independent
  checks ([](#sec-7-1), [](#sec-10-3)); (iii) the claim that this version's
  producer bytes are unchanged is explicitly *not* made — two producer-side
  narrowings are disclosed above, each travelling a legible per-artifact
  refusal path; and (iv) the 2.1.0 entry below is corrected in place.
- **Fixtures: 58 total (21 valid, 37 invalid), up from 44.** The
  unknown-member notice fixture moved from the invalid set to the valid set
  and was extended with an unrecognized member inside `message`; valid
  unknown-member fixtures were added for result and inbound-message; the
  withheld-body invalid fixture was deleted, having become schema-valid
  under the relaxed envelope (its name always over-claimed what
  `additionalProperties` alone tested); the bad-provenance fixture moved to
  the valid set as an unrecognized-value case; an invalid extra-member
  submit-request fixture was added to pin the closed side of the asymmetry,
  previously untested at the top level; four invalid fixtures were added
  for the address-syntax and control-character rules; a traversal
  `notice_id` fixture was added; the `content_ref` index-mismatch fixture
  was added, and is the one invalid `notice-*` case that is schema-valid on
  its own; and six fixtures pin the trailing-newline anchoring case.

## 2.3.0 — inbound byte access in force
{: numbered="false"}

A **minor bump**, prose only: no schema rule and no fixture changed, and
the wire major stayed `"2"`. It resolved an inconsistency carried since
2.0.0. A fixture showing a `clean` Descriptor with `content_ref` and
`sha256` sat in the valid set as a deliberately forward-valid shape, while
the prose forbade any Runtime from producing it — the gate blessed what
the prose forbade. This version sided with the fixtures: the
release-scoped byte embargo was dropped, and the standing rule became the
one stated in [](#sec-7-2) — a Runtime may publish bytes and emit
`content_ref`/`sha256` only for a Descriptor it asserts `clean`, and a
Runtime that cannot make that determination must not assert `clean`. The
substantive clarification is that `disposition` is the Runtime's
*assertion*, its basis deliberately unspecified; the older prose presumed
a scanner, which is what made byte access read as a deferred capability
rather than as a policy decision whose wire shape major 2 already carried.
Nothing was tightened, and there is still no outbound `content_ref`.

## 2.2.0 — deletion rights, read-only inbound
{: numbered="false"}

A **permissive minor bump**: no wire member, no schema change, no fixture
changed or added. It (a) declared a read-only inbound tree a valid Runtime
posture and made the inbound `processed/` archive directory optional, (b)
obliged a Connector not to require inbound write access, making
consumption-recording Connector-private, and (c) named deletion rights per
artifact class, along with the two constraints attached to them: a
Connector must not delete or rewrite a published request, and a Connector
exercising its new deletion rights must preserve `req_id` monotonicity by
other durable means. It also pinned three previously implicit
`reason_code` rules: setting one on every Result became a keyworded
SHOULD, and a consumer must tolerate an unrecognized code and must not
infer `outcome` from it.

## 2.1.0 — the body spool, and three optional Result members
{: numbered="false"}

Recorded at the time as an **additive minor bump**: one new optional
spooled artifact (the Inbound Message) and its schema, one new optional
Notice member (`provenance`), and three new optional Result members
(`reason_code`, `attestation`, `ts`). No existing schema gained a required
member, no existing fixture changed shape, and the wire major stayed `"2"`.

**Correction (recorded at 3.0.0).** That "additive minor" label was
unsound as written. Against a strictly validating 2.0.0 consumer — every
schema at that version was closed at every level — a Notice carrying the
new `provenance` member failed schema validation as a **whole document**
and was silently dropped: precisely the failure mode cited as the reason
attachments had required a major bump, reintroduced as a minor. Version
3.0.0 repairs the versioning model rather than relabelling this history,
which is why this entry still records what shipped rather than what would
have been correct.

## 2.0.0 — attachments, and `contract_version` required
{: numbered="false"}

A **major bump**, moving the wire major from `"1"` to `"2"`. It added
attachment support in both directions — outbound Descriptors with required
`size_bytes` and `sha256` and their Sidecar bytes, inbound Descriptors with
a `disposition` — and made `contract_version` REQUIRED on the
submit-request, deliver-notice, and result artifacts, with no
compatibility mode. The reason the bump had to be major rather than minor
is the reasoning in [](#sec-10-1): under the then-universal closed
envelopes, a new member caused a major-1 consumer to reject the *whole
document*, which inbound is silent total message loss. A version signal,
rather than an unknown-member rejection, is what makes that refusal
legible.

## Fixture identifier normalization
{: numbered="false"}

Across these versions the fixture suite's identities were normalized to
`example.com` and `example.org` placeholders, and deployment-specific
identifiers were removed from fixture content. This changed no shape and
no expectation: every affected fixture validates exactly as before, and no
identifier removed in that pass is reintroduced by this document.
